{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And HaShem said to me: Do not harass Moav.</b> To me He gave warning concerning all of them, because I was destined to be buried in the plains of Moav. Concerning these three nations — Seir, Ammon, and Moav — the Holy One, blessed be He, warned Israel not to conquer their land. Seir: because we find that they were called Hivites, as it is written: ״ואת אהליבמה בת ענה בת צבעון החוי״. And an opponent has grounds to argue and say that this is the Hivite listed among the seven nations given to Israel. Therefore Scripture writes here: ״ובשעיר ישבו החורים לפנים״ — meaning, their original name was Hori, and they are not among the seven nations. And the reason they were called Hivite is because they sniff out the land like a snake, saying: this plot is good for vines, and this one for date-palms. <b>Moav:</b> because its land is called the land of Rephaim, as it is written: ״רפאים יחשבו אף הם״, and there are grounds to say that it was given to Avraham, as it is written in the portion of לך לך: ״ואת הרפאים״, so Israel has a claim to conquer them. Therefore Scripture writes here: ״והמואבים יקראו להם אימים״ — meaning, this was their original name, and they are not the Rephaim given to Avraham, and Israel has no claim to their land. Ammon: likewise they were called Rephaim, as it is written: ״ארץ רפאים תחשב אף היא״, and so that one should not say about them that they are permitted to Israel as we said, ״ואת הרפאים״ — Scripture writes here: <b>and the Ammonites call them Zamzumim</b> — meaning, this was their original name, and they are not the Rephaim given to Avraham. Another interpretation: concerning these three nations they were warned because they were given to Avraham in the covenant between the pieces, as it is written: ״את הקיני ואת הקנזי ואת הקדמוני״ — and these are Ammon, Moav, and Edom — so that Israel should not say about them: behold, they are permitted to us, for they are among the ten nations given to Avraham. Therefore it says: ״ונשמרתם מאד״ etc., for their measure is not yet full, and they were not permitted to Israel until the days of the Messiah, as Rashi explained. But the other nations, against which Israel had no claim whatsoever, Israel did not need to be warned about them — why would conquering them even come to mind?" "<b>I have given Ar as an inheritance.</b> Sichon took it, as it is written: ״אכלה ער מואב״, and it returned to Moav at the very end."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The closing idiom לכבשן is rendered hyper-literally; the intended meaning per the idiom לסק אדעתיה is 'why would it even come to mind.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:51:30.682183035Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}